-- The accumulation rule: what a customer does, and what it is worth.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand. It
-- is the only SQL this feature ships.
--
-- ## The hole these two columns close
--
-- `earn_rate` already existed and already had a default of `1.0`. It was a rate
-- with nothing to be a rate *of*, and nothing in the system converted money into
-- points:
--
--   -- workers/growth/src/routes.ts, before
--   const effectivePoints = Math.floor(body.points * earnRate * tierMultiplier);
--
-- `body.points` is already points, and it is computed by whoever calls the route.
-- So the merchant-facing config screen ("1 punto por cada $1.000 pesos de
-- compra") described a rule the backend did not implement, and the caller — not
-- the tenant's configuration — decided every balance. `docs/17-Estrategia-
-- Competitiva.md` §7.5 settles who holds the rule: "A. El Growth, con la regla
-- en config del tenant". This migration is the schema half of that decision.
--
-- ## Why two columns and not one
--
-- Because `earn_rate` cannot mean the same thing in both mechanics, and saying so
-- is the whole point of the pair:
--
--   earn_by = 'visit'    -> earn_rate is points per qualifying count. The caller
--                            supplies the count, because "9 cafes y el 10 gratis"
--                            has no amount attached to it.
--   earn_by = 'purchase' -> earn_rate is points per `earn_unit_amount` pesos. The
--                            caller supplies the amount and the points are
--                            derived here, never sent in.
--
-- Collapsing this into one number would have kept exactly the ambiguity this
-- feature exists to remove.
--
-- ## Why 'visit' is the default, and why it is NOT NULL
--
-- 'visit' is the mechanic the earn route already implements, and the one an
-- honest integration supports: with a manual scanner there is no amount, only
-- the fact that a visit happened. Defaulting to 'purchase' would have made every
-- existing caller's request start failing with AMOUNT_REQUIRED the moment this
-- landed, which is a rule change disguised as a schema change.
--
-- `NOT NULL` because the earn route has to be able to branch on this value
-- without a null case. A nullable `earn_by` would mean one more place where
-- "unconfigured" silently reads as some mechanic, and this one decides whether a
-- caller-supplied point count is trusted.
--
-- ## Why earn_unit_amount defaults to 1000.00
--
-- Two reasons, and they point the same way.
--
-- First, the default has to be a number a program can actually run: `NULL` here
-- would mean "earns points from money but has not said how much money", which is
-- a division by zero in the middle of a sale rather than a configuration error at
-- the moment it was written. `NOT NULL` removes that state.
--
-- Second, 1.000 is the value the merchant-facing screen was already displaying,
-- so a program that switches to `purchase` without touching the unit keeps paying
-- what the merchant was shown. That is a default chosen to match a promise that
-- was already on the screen — NOT an endorsement of "1 punto por cada $1.000" as
-- a sensible rate. The Growth route refuses a `purchase` switch that does not
-- carry its own `earn_unit_amount`, precisely so this default can never be
-- mistaken for a decision.
--
-- `numeric(12,2)` rather than an integer: pesos are currency and the unit is a
-- money amount, so it has to hold cents without a lossy conversion. Two decimals
-- also keep a whole-peso unit (`1000.00`) exactly representable, which an integer
-- column plus a scale change would not.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
ALTER TABLE "loyalty_programs" ADD COLUMN "earn_by" text DEFAULT 'visit' NOT NULL;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "loyalty_programs" ADD COLUMN "earn_unit_amount" numeric(12, 2) DEFAULT '1000.00' NOT NULL;